  faqHeading: "SEO cost in the UAE: the questions buyers actually ask",
  faqs: [
    { question: "How much does SEO cost in the UAE?", answer: `There is no single figure because you are buying labour hours, and market rates span more than an order of magnitude depending on overhead, geography and seniority. The useful approach is to convert any quote into hours at a plausible blended rate and check the promised deliverables fit inside them. Our own monthly work starts from ${P.seo}.` },
    { question: "Why is SEO charged monthly instead of as a one-off project?", answer: "Because a ranking is a position relative to competitors who keep working, in an index that is continuously re-evaluated, on a site that keeps changing. Stopping does not undo what was built — pages, fixes and links stay yours — but it does stop new progress while others continue. Some SEO work genuinely is a one-off project, and if your budget is small that is usually the better buy." },
    { question: "How do I compare two SEO quotes with very different prices?", answer: "Divide each fee by a plausible blended hourly rate to get the hours it buys, then estimate the hours the promised deliverables actually require. Ask both suppliers for the hour split between senior, developer, writer and junior time, and whether the monthly report will state hours spent. That converts two incomparable documents into one comparable number." },
    { question: "What is a realistic minimum SEO budget for a UAE business?", answer: "Low enough and the fee funds tool subscriptions and reporting rather than work on your site, because those costs exist before anything is optimised. If your available budget sits near that floor, buy a defined one-off project — a technical fix programme, or a properly built set of pages — and own the result, rather than a thin retainer you will cancel in month three." },
    { question: "How long before SEO shows results in the UAE?", answer: "Expect four to six months before non-brand data is readable, and longer on a new domain or in a saturated vertical. There are five lags in sequence: crawling, re-evaluation, ranking movement, impressions and then clicks. Technical fixes on a large site can show in weeks. A commercial head term in a competitive UAE sector can take two quarters to settle, or may not be winnable." },
    { question: "Can any SEO company in the UAE guarantee first-page rankings?", answer: "No. Nobody can guarantee a position, including us, because rankings are produced by systems no supplier controls and respond to competitors as much as to your site. Guarantees that do exist are usually structured around keywords the supplier picked, an unspecified location or device, or a refund clause requiring perfect client implementation. Treat any position guarantee as a reason to stop reading." },
    { question: "What does a cheap SEO retainer leave out?", answer: "Implementation first, so you receive recommendations nobody deploys. Then editing, so content exists but is not good. Then link outreach, replaced by nothing or by purchased placements. Then analysis, replaced by a dashboard. Then Arabic query research, which is invisible unless you ask. And finally senior thinking, which is what decides whether the plan was right at all." },
    { question: "How do I audit an SEO invoice?", answer: "For each billed line, name the artefact that proves it: a URL with a date, a changed page source, a redirect you can test in a browser, your own Search Console data, a profile edit history. Sample three at random each month. Also require the hours spent and one decision made. An artefact that cannot be produced is a line you should stop paying for." },
    { question: "Who should own my Search Console and analytics accounts?", answer: "You, in your company's name, with the supplier added as a removable collaborator. Properties created inside a supplier's own account leave when they do, taking the historical data that makes future measurement possible. Agree this before signature; it is free then and expensive later. It is also the single thing that makes an invoice auditable at all." },
    { question: "Is SEO cheaper than Google Ads in the UAE?", answer: "Per enquiry, sometimes, and later. Work out cost per non-brand organic click by dividing the fee by non-brand clicks, then compare it with your paid cost per click in the same queries. Paid produces enquiries this week and stops when you stop; search work compounds and lags by months. If you need customers this quarter, paid is the correct channel and we will say so." },
    { question: "What happens to my rankings if I stop paying for SEO?", answer: "They do not collapse. Pages, technical fixes, speed improvements, internal linking and earned links stay with your domain. What stops is new work, so position erodes gradually as competitors continue — a plateau and then a slow decline rather than a cliff. What you can genuinely lose is access to accounts and historical data held in a supplier's name, which is a contract issue, not a search one." },
    { question: "Does an SEO invoice from outside the UAE include VAT?", answer: "A UAE-registered supplier adds 5%, which a VAT-registered client generally recovers, so compare net figures rather than gross ones. An invoice from outside the UAE is an import of services and is treated differently, with your tax adviser confirming how it affects your return. We state the mechanics and never advise on them, and we invoice in USD from India." },
    { question: "How many hours of work should I expect for my monthly fee?", answer: "Ask the supplier rather than accepting an industry average, and insist the figure is in the proposal. Then check the split: senior diagnosis, developer implementation, writing and editing, and junior execution are four different costs and four different values. A retainer that is almost all junior hours can be good value for volume work and poor value for a site whose problem is structural." },
    { question: "Should I pay for links as part of an SEO budget?", answer: "Pay for the hours of outreach, not for the placements. Outreach is work with a low success rate and no photogenic output, which is why thin retainers replace it with purchased links that transfer risk to your domain to make a report look full. Ask directly whether any placements are paid for, and ask to see the live linking pages." },
    { question: "When is SEO the wrong spend for a UAE business?", answer: "When you need customers this quarter, when your positioning is still changing weekly, when nobody can answer enquiries quickly, when the real problem is a site that cannot be fixed cheaply, when your budget sits below the cost floor, and when your catchment is one neighbourhood and a well-run Google Business Profile plus a few local pages would do more for less." },
    { question: "Why are you cheaper than a Dubai SEO agency?", answer: "Because our costs are lower, not because we found an efficiency they missed. We are three freelance engineers in India with no office, no UAE trade licence, no UAE phone number, no sales team and no account management layer. What you give up is local presence, local legal recourse, a dirham invoice, and any bench to surge if your project suddenly needs ten people." },
    { question: "What should I send to get a real price rather than a range?", answer: "Your domain, read-only access to Search Console and analytics, the queries you believe matter commercially, whether Arabic is in scope, who can approve content and how quickly, and who can deploy changes if we cannot. With those we can give you a baseline, a diagnosis, and either a scope with hours in it or a recommendation not to buy monthly work at all." },
  ],
